1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Is it really fireproof?

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:07,000
Crashproof?

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,000
Weatherproof?

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:13,940
Can it hold up to a sledgehammer?

5
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:15,940
A ton of dynamite?

6
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,960
A really big sneeze?

7
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, crash testing on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:32,000
We'll be right back.

9
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:56,560
In the universe of crash testing,

10
00:00:56,561 --> 00:00:59,960
Nothing gets smashed, flipped, skidded, rolled,

11
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:04,500
and generally banged up as much as cars.

12
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,300
The government, insurance companies, consumer groups,

13
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:10,380
and most of all, car manufacturers

14
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:14,080
take literally thousands of shiny new vehicles each year

15
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,340
and total them in a spectacular variety of ways.

16
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:24,560
Government-band-aided frontal side and rear impact tasks

17
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:27,060
are just the beginning.

18
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:30,300
Car makers have staged and tested an amazing range

19
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:38,100
of accident scenarios, including this test by Volvo,

20
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:41,580
in which a car was actually driven off a second story roof.

21
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:50,120
Cameras inside the vehicle record the plunge

22
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:51,580
from the driver's point of view.

23
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:58,800
In addition to crashes, of course, manufacturers have put cars

24
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:01,200
through a great variety of performance tests,

25
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:07,080
bouncing them along grueling roads, through water,

26
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:09,940
across snow and ice.

27
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:13,240
The history of such tests goes back to the early days

28
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:15,380
of the auto industry.

29
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:19,580
In 1924, General Motors began developing a vast proving

30
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:24,560
ground on a 4,000-acre tract of land north of Detroit.

31
00:02:24,561 --> 00:02:28,000
The site they were looking for needed to have both water

32
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:32,100
on the facility to do some water intrusion testing.

33
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:34,140
Plus, it needed areas where we could construct

34
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,380
straightaways, hill climbs.

35
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:38,940
And the site here in Melford had enough difference

36
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,180
in elevation that we could basically construct

37
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:45,340
any type of a road that a consumer might encounter

38
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:48,120
in a real world driving situation.

39
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:49,980
And some that a real world driver

40
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:53,360
would never encounter.

41
00:02:53,361 --> 00:02:55,960
Early performance tests were usually simple

42
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:58,360
and frequently clever.

43
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:01,540
We had people pouring water on the tire

44
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,540
as we went around in a circle to measure turning radius.

45
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:06,440
We also had some large pendulums

46
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:08,300
that we would pull cars onto.

47
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,480
And by measuring the timing of the pendulum,

48
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:14,380
we could determine the center of gravity of the vehicle.

49
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:16,820
We did roll over testing of vehicles

50
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:19,880
where we had an elevated ramp and we'd pull a car up

51
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:22,520
the elevated ramp and let it roll over a hill

52
00:03:22,521 --> 00:03:26,120
to measure structural integrity of the vehicle.

53
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:28,960
We've got footage of crash testing

54
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:31,060
where the operator would stand on the running boards

55
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:33,300
and put a brick on the accelerator.

56
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,300
And just before he hit the wall, he

57
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:38,000
jumped into the arms of a couple of waiting fellows

58
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,440
who caught him while the car cleaned into the wall.

59
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:46,020
Safety issues came to the fore in the 1950s

60
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:49,380
with the arrival of faster, deadlier cars.

61
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:51,880
As a result, crash testing kicked into gear

62
00:03:51,881 --> 00:03:53,780
with car makers putting their vehicles

63
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:58,380
through a much wider range of tests.

64
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:01,060
The introduction of anthropomorphic test

65
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:03,860
devices, otherwise known as crash test dummies,

66
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,460
pushed car crash testing to another level.

67
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:10,940
CTDs, as they are called, were first developed after World War

68
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:14,340
II by the Air Force to test ejection seats,

69
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:19,840
or as in this footage, for pressurized cabin tests.

70
00:04:19,841 --> 00:04:23,880
This pioneering dummy was built in 1946.

71
00:04:23,980 --> 00:04:27,180
It wasn't much, but it was better than a cadaver,

72
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:30,580
which is what was sometimes used in early tests.

73
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:32,380
The first generations of dummies

74
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:37,700
were basically mannequins, stiff, rigid, not

75
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:41,200
instrumentatable, where we could get a lot of data off

76
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:44,940
them, simply more mass in a vehicle,

77
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,200
and very limited into how lifelike it was

78
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:49,440
and the actual response.

79
00:04:49,441 --> 00:04:52,840
The evolution of dummies quickened in the 1960s,

80
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:54,840
especially after the federal government

81
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:59,020
required automakers to safety test all new cars.

82
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:01,880
A new generation of CTDs emerged,

83
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:04,220
dummies modeled on the human skeleton

84
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:07,260
and increasingly able to replicate the biomechanics

85
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,560
of people.

86
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:10,160
The biofidelity of these crash dummies

87
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:11,800
is quite remarkable.

88
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,800
They very closely simulate and duplicate

89
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:16,740
the actual motion and kinematics of a real person

90
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:17,740
in a crash.

91
00:05:17,741 --> 00:05:19,880
An example is a chest cavity.

92
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:22,620
The neck is designed to rotate and flex the same way

93
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:25,080
a human neck would rotate or flex.

94
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:29,520
The femurs are designed to duplicate real human femurs

95
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:31,020
during the cramp.

96
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:33,060
3, 2, 1.

97
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:39,560
The bio fidelity of dummies is so crucial to safety testing

98
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:42,540
that the dummies must themselves be tested, whacked

99
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:46,540
in the chest with a 50-pound weight at 15 miles per hour

100
00:05:46,541 --> 00:05:51,580
to make sure their rib cages respond just like the person's.

101
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:53,940
Instrumentation was the other big breakthrough

102
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:55,340
in dummy development.

103
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:57,980
Early dummies were stand-ins for people.

104
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:01,020
Contemporary dummies are extraordinary tools packed

105
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:04,300
from head to toe with an incredible thicket of as many

106
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:08,300
as 100 sensors, including accelerometers, which

107
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:12,540
measure speed, and load cells, which measure impact.

108
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:14,100
We have accelerometers on the head

109
00:06:14,101 --> 00:06:18,400
to measure the acceleration of the head during the crash.

110
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:20,740
We also have load cells at various points of the dummy,

111
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:23,140
including the upper neck, the lower neck.

112
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:26,140
We've got accelerometers in the rib cage, on the sternum,

113
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:27,340
and also on the spine.

114
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:29,940
In the pelvic region, we have load cells in the lumbar

115
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:33,460
region, in the leg region, the knees, and also the tibias.

116
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:35,460
Not surprisingly, these dummies

117
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:41,060
have a high-tech price tag upwards of $100,000 apiece,

118
00:06:41,061 --> 00:06:45,260
which is why General Motors has a dummy hospital, where CTDs

119
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:48,460
are examined and patched up after each crash

120
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:51,300
and readied for the next assignment.

121
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:55,800
In contrast to the freewheeling crash tests of the early days,

122
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:59,520
tests today are prepared with extraordinary care.

123
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:03,720
Sensors record as many as 200 points of impact,

124
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:06,660
and each one must be wired to channels that carry the

125
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:08,820
information to analysts.

126
00:07:08,821 --> 00:07:12,260
Between 20 and 30 cameras must also be readied in order

127
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:14,860
to adequately document the event.

128
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:18,860
Crash testing is really a fairly unforgiving business.

129
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:20,860
We don't have a lot of second chances here.

130
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:22,560
Clearly, it's destructive testing,

131
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:24,660
and the test specimen is the vehicle.

132
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:27,380
And when we test a vehicle, tow a vehicle into the wall

133
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:30,480
at 30 or 40 miles an hour, the sole purpose of doing that

134
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:32,680
is to collect information about that crash test.

135
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:34,620
And you don't get the information you're after.

136
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:36,420
You've just lost a significant investment.

137
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:41,280
Especially if the car being crashed

138
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:45,920
is a one-of-a-kind prototype, which has to pass a safety test

139
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:49,000
before it can be approved for mass production.

140
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:54,140
The prototype alone can cost over $1 million.

141
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:57,140
Crashing planes, of course, is even more expensive

142
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:00,600
than crashing cars, especially when they don't hit the ground

143
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:01,540
as planned.

144
00:08:30,057 --> 00:08:34,480
Aircraft, like automobiles, are also crash tested in a variety of ways.

145
00:08:34,481 --> 00:08:37,660
Blown up, dropped, slammed into walls.

146
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:43,300
The military has been crashing planes since the 1920s.

147
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:46,400
This twin prop antique was pushed down a long ramp

148
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:50,660
and into a wall in order to pinpoint when and how it would

149
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:53,840
burst into flames.

150
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:56,200
High speed cameras enabled researchers

151
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,600
to study the fireball, which ignited exactly three

152
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:02,440
fifths of a second after impact.

153
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:05,780
It wasn't until the late 1950s when airplanes and jets

154
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:08,320
became a truly popular form of travel

155
00:09:08,321 --> 00:09:10,720
that the Federal Aviation Administration began

156
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:13,360
to safety crash planes.

157
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:16,560
One large-scale early test involved this four propeller

158
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:20,640
super-constellation, which the FAA smashed into a hillside

159
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:22,940
in the Arizona desert in 1964.

160
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:31,080
Interestingly, the FAA has almost never crash tested a plane

161
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:32,580
from the air.

162
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:35,880
The one exception occurred in 1984,

163
00:09:35,881 --> 00:09:39,360
when a remote-controlled four-engine Boeing 720

164
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:41,360
took off from Edwards Air Force Base

165
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:45,160
in California's Mojave Desert.

166
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:49,460
It was the only time that we remotely flew an aircraft.

167
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:54,340
It is extremely, extremely difficult to do that.

168
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:56,360
The expense certainly is one thing.

169
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:59,600
The complications of just the safety issues alone,

170
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:02,080
we had to be sure that if something went wrong with that

171
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:04,840
remote-control operation, that we literally

172
00:10:04,841 --> 00:10:07,480
could explode that aircraft over the desert,

173
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:09,380
not over populated areas.

174
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:12,720
Four years of preparation and $12 million,

175
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:16,620
not counting the jumbo jet, went into this one of a kind

176
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:18,280
test.

177
00:10:18,380 --> 00:10:21,920
Over 90 crash test dummies were buckled into the plane,

178
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:25,560
and incredible 300 motion picture cameras, some of them

179
00:10:25,660 --> 00:10:31,000
on the fuselage itself, were positioned to record the event.

180
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:34,200
The FAA was going all out because it wanted to test

181
00:10:34,201 --> 00:10:37,100
a fuel additive that it thought might prevent some planes

182
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:40,580
from generating deadly fireballs when they crash.

183
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:44,040
Fireballs normally erupt when a plane's fuel tanks

184
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:47,020
are ripped open so violently that the fuel whips out

185
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:51,020
in a mist that ignites on contact with flame.

186
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:55,680
Anti-misting formula called AMK had proven effective in a series

187
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:59,320
of small-scale tests, and the FAA wanted to test it

188
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:02,500
on a full-blown crash before ordering the airline industry

189
00:11:02,501 --> 00:11:05,640
to put the additive in all aviation fuel.

190
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:07,940
But the test was very tricky.

191
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:10,980
The FAA had to make sure the jet crashed in a way

192
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:14,620
that would make the fuel spew and mist.

193
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:17,780
To that end, crash engineers placed eight wing opening

194
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:21,820
devices at precise points on the crash path of the plane,

195
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:27,200
designed to slash the wing fuel tanks and trigger the mist.

196
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:29,300
To make sure the mist had a chance to ignite,

197
00:11:29,301 --> 00:11:32,900
the FAA placed kerosene jets in the tail of the plane,

198
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,080
set to fire for nine seconds after the crash.

199
00:11:37,180 --> 00:11:41,340
12 high-intensity light towers directly in the impact zone

200
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:43,840
and a landing bed made of broken rock designed

201
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:46,220
to violently vibrate the plane were

202
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:49,760
added to facilitate ignition.

203
00:11:49,860 --> 00:11:54,520
Ignition, as it turned out, was the least of the FAA's worries.

204
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:56,760
The remote-controlled craft hit the ground

205
00:11:56,761 --> 00:11:59,860
at an unplanned angle, just enough off target

206
00:11:59,960 --> 00:12:03,200
to rip into the fuel supply in a way that did not create any

207
00:12:03,300 --> 00:12:06,400
mist, but did send a geyser of fuel

208
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,840
into a burning engine, creating just what the FAA hoped it

209
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:14,240
wouldn't see, a spectacular fireball.

210
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:18,580
Frankly, it did not go as well as we had predicted.

211
00:12:18,680 --> 00:12:22,260
The attitude of that plane was not typical of a landing.

212
00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:25,420
Had a pilot been on board for a normal landing

213
00:12:25,421 --> 00:12:27,820
at an airport, the plane would have gone around.

214
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:30,760
He would have applied power and gone around a second time

215
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:31,860
and made the landing.

216
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:35,560
Of course, the way the fuel came out of the tanks,

217
00:12:35,660 --> 00:12:39,360
most of it not misting, but simply literally pouring out

218
00:12:39,460 --> 00:12:43,900
as a fuel, pouring on an engine that was on fire,

219
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,900
it didn't even have the opportunity

220
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,280
to demonstrate its capability to prevent misting.

221
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:52,620
We could not prove the concept that AMK worked.

222
00:12:52,621 --> 00:12:55,620
Although the AMK part of the test went awry,

223
00:12:55,720 --> 00:12:59,660
and the FAA never did recommend the additive for general use,

224
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:03,400
this landmark test was successful in many ways.

225
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:05,440
The cameras and dummies in the cockpit,

226
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:08,900
as well as the passenger section, gave FAA analysts

227
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,740
a chillingly realistic sense of what

228
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:14,700
it's like to be inside a plane when it crash lands

229
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:17,080
and goes up in flames.

230
00:13:17,180 --> 00:13:19,320
A great deal of important safety data

231
00:13:19,420 --> 00:13:21,040
was gleaned from the test.

232
00:13:21,041 --> 00:13:24,140
information about the behavior of overhead compartments,

233
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:27,640
seat structures, hazardous materials containers,

234
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:29,820
and other components.

235
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:31,780
The instrumented dummies, meanwhile,

236
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:35,120
provided exact data on the stress loads a person would

237
00:13:35,220 --> 00:13:37,360
experience in a crash.

238
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:40,300
We can measure the forces from the impact starting

239
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:43,560
at the ground level through the frame of an aircraft,

240
00:13:43,660 --> 00:13:47,360
through the seat of an aircraft, and into the individual itself.

241
00:13:47,460 --> 00:13:49,440
You don't want people incapacitated

242
00:13:49,441 --> 00:13:51,240
that they can't get out of the aircraft.

243
00:13:51,340 --> 00:13:53,240
The question is, what are those forces?

244
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:56,640
How strong should the seats be in order to protect the human

245
00:13:56,740 --> 00:13:58,440
in case of an accident?

246
00:13:58,540 --> 00:14:00,580
Although the FAA has yet to attempt

247
00:14:00,680 --> 00:14:04,680
another air to ground crash, it does simulate such crashes

248
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:09,600
by combining data from two other kinds of tests.

249
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:12,940
A vertical drop test in which a fully mocked up passenger

250
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:16,640
section of a plane is dropped from 12 feet,

251
00:14:16,641 --> 00:14:20,380
and horizontal ground tests in which the same kind of section

252
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:22,980
is rammed into a concrete wall.

253
00:14:23,080 --> 00:14:25,580
If you add those two vectors together,

254
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:29,380
they are simulating what would occur in an aircraft that

255
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:35,900
might impact the ground at, let's say, 120 knots landing.

256
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,360
The actual forces on the body would be those two vectors

257
00:14:39,460 --> 00:14:41,200
put together.

258
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:46,640
The FAA also does a great deal of fire testing.

259
00:14:46,641 --> 00:14:53,640
and as part of its terrorism defense program,

260
00:14:53,641 --> 00:14:56,640
blows up an occasional plane.

261
00:14:56,641 --> 00:15:02,640
Also blowing up planes, the Department of Defense,

262
00:15:02,641 --> 00:15:05,640
which has been conducting vulnerability and lethality

263
00:15:05,641 --> 00:15:09,640
tests on its fleet since the Second World War.

264
00:15:09,641 --> 00:15:13,640
This entire field at the Aberdeen Proving Ground in Maryland

265
00:15:13,641 --> 00:15:16,940
is filled with helicopters and planes that have been

266
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:20,080
or will be blown up and destroyed.

267
00:15:20,180 --> 00:15:25,180
I had the seventh largest air force in the world at one time.

268
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:30,260
We currently have on inventory here at Air Base Range 6

269
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:33,220
a variety of helicopters and fixed wing aircraft.

270
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:39,300
These aircraft have been brought here to be destroyed.

271
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:41,260
When it comes to destruction,

272
00:15:41,261 --> 00:15:44,700
No plane has ever been totaled in a crash test more thoroughly

273
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:48,260
than this one, an F-4 Phantom sent hurtling along

274
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:53,000
a 2,000-foot sled track at 480 miles per hour.

275
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:57,240
The 43,000-pound plane literally dematerialized

276
00:15:57,340 --> 00:16:01,380
as it plowed into a 1 million pound, 12-foot thick concrete

277
00:16:01,480 --> 00:16:06,620
wall, leaving behind nothing but dust and rubble.

278
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:08,620
The test commissioned by the Japanese

279
00:16:08,621 --> 00:16:11,380
was designed to see if the concrete casing that houses

280
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:14,920
nuclear power plants would stand up to a direct hit

281
00:16:15,020 --> 00:16:17,020
by a falling jet.

282
00:16:17,120 --> 00:16:20,560
Though the jet disintegrated, damage to the concrete wall

283
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:21,360
was minor.

284
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:28,480
In the world of product testing,

285
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:31,780
few items are challenged quite as seriously as those that are

286
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:35,480
supposed to safeguard our money.

287
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:37,420
This man is trying to torch his way

288
00:16:37,421 --> 00:16:40,160
into the vault of an ATM machine.

289
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:42,200
He works for underwriters laboratories,

290
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:46,200
and his job is to attack ATM saves, deposit boxes,

291
00:16:46,300 --> 00:16:49,200
and bank vaults with the tools and the mentality

292
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:51,640
of a hardcore burglar.

293
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:53,740
How they would think, how they would act,

294
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:56,900
we assume they have very intimate knowledge of the product

295
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,580
and would know exactly where to drill, where to pound,

296
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:03,180
where the money would be stored in the container.

297
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:05,740
The goal of the test is to remove the contents

298
00:17:05,741 --> 00:17:10,120
or 10% of the contents within a specific time

299
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:13,380
using very specific tools.

300
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:16,480
Underwriters Laboratories has been torching and pounding

301
00:17:16,580 --> 00:17:19,680
safes and vaults since the 1920s,

302
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:23,500
testing them for fire as well as theft.

303
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:27,460
After burning a safe for four hours at 2,000 degrees,

304
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:31,200
UL would hoist it 30 feet and drop it onto a basement floor

305
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:35,140
covered with brick rubble to simulate a safe collapsing

306
00:17:35,141 --> 00:17:37,540
between floors.

307
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:40,740
Imagine dropping a safe weighing as much as five tons

308
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:41,640
three stories.

309
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:43,480
It shakes our building, literally.

310
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:47,080
So that's a very extreme test to pass.

311
00:17:47,180 --> 00:17:50,580
Underwriters Laboratories was created over a century ago

312
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:54,600
by a young electrical engineer named William Henry Merrill,

313
00:17:54,700 --> 00:17:57,900
who visited the Palace of Electricity at the 1893

314
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Chicago World's Fair and was both thrilled and appalled

315
00:18:02,100 --> 00:18:03,400
by what he saw.

316
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:05,140
He said, you know, I think these electrical

317
00:18:05,141 --> 00:18:06,040
are going to catch on.

318
00:18:06,041 --> 00:18:07,280
I think they're going to be big.

319
00:18:07,281 --> 00:18:10,080
But the problem is, if they burn down all the buildings,

320
00:18:10,081 --> 00:18:12,780
you're going to incur a lot of losses as insurance companies.

321
00:18:12,781 --> 00:18:14,980
Why don't we set up a lab to test the products

322
00:18:14,981 --> 00:18:16,520
before they go into the buildings?

323
00:18:16,521 --> 00:18:19,580
That way, they'll be safe, and you won't incur the losses.

324
00:18:19,581 --> 00:18:21,380
Backed by the insurance companies

325
00:18:21,381 --> 00:18:23,500
that were underwriting electrical goods,

326
00:18:23,501 --> 00:18:26,760
Merrill opened a one-room testing lab above a fire

327
00:18:26,761 --> 00:18:31,200
station, fittingly enough, in 1894.

328
00:18:31,201 --> 00:18:33,660
Over the next few decades, UL Safety

329
00:18:33,661 --> 00:18:37,560
tested an ever-expanding range of goods, everything

330
00:18:37,660 --> 00:18:44,200
from lightning rods to hair curlers

331
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:45,500
to bulletproof glass.

332
00:18:48,940 --> 00:18:52,940
This emergency flare had to work after being held underwater

333
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:54,980
for two minutes.

334
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:58,520
These irons were left on for up to 500 hours,

335
00:18:58,620 --> 00:19:01,780
then drop tested five times.

336
00:19:01,781 --> 00:19:08,480
This electric blanket was washed while plugged in and turned on.

337
00:19:08,580 --> 00:19:12,620
25 backfires were enough to pop this muffler and cost it

338
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:14,420
a UL seal.

339
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:17,420
And without the seal, most major insurance companies

340
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:20,340
would not back a new product.

341
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:23,900
By the 1940s, UL was testing virtually everything,

342
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,880
even tear gas designed to stop someone from robbing a bank,

343
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:31,280
or this odd money bag stuffed with firecrackers

344
00:19:31,281 --> 00:19:33,980
that went off if the bag was jerked and grabbed.

345
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:43,420
TVs, of course, had to be tested to make sure

346
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:45,860
they wouldn't expel dangerous bits of glass

347
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:47,360
if hit by a foreign object.

348
00:19:50,660 --> 00:19:53,800
When a tube like this implodes, it's almost synonymous

349
00:19:53,900 --> 00:19:55,880
to a grenade going off in your living room.

350
00:19:55,980 --> 00:19:58,780
It expels all kinds of shrapnel or glass

351
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:00,240
over a very large area.

352
00:20:00,241 --> 00:20:01,940
on your whole living room floor

353
00:20:01,941 --> 00:20:04,080
all the way to the corners of your living room.

354
00:20:04,081 --> 00:20:10,440
The only thing left of the tube is basically just this.

355
00:20:11,960 --> 00:20:14,940
The glass is everywhere except on the tube.

356
00:20:16,820 --> 00:20:21,000
UL smashes 18 picture tubes of each new model TV

357
00:20:21,001 --> 00:20:23,280
to make sure it's got a fair sampling.

358
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:27,620
After each test, the debris is carefully measured.

359
00:20:27,621 --> 00:20:31,420
If more than 42 grams of glass blow out past this line,

360
00:20:31,520 --> 00:20:34,720
the TV fails.

361
00:20:34,820 --> 00:20:38,560
Even toys are subject to the attacks of UL engineers.

362
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:46,380
What we're going to do is apply 30 pounds of force

363
00:20:46,480 --> 00:20:49,680
directly on the eye.

364
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:51,720
This doll's head is being pressed

365
00:20:51,820 --> 00:20:54,420
to see whether the eyes or something else a child

366
00:20:54,520 --> 00:20:56,880
could swallow pop out.

367
00:20:56,881 --> 00:20:59,880
Well, you know, it may look a little bit different and gross.

368
00:20:59,980 --> 00:21:03,420
It may look like we're being torturous and inhumane,

369
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:05,320
but it's all in the line of safety

370
00:21:05,420 --> 00:21:07,620
and the different requirements of the standard.

371
00:21:07,720 --> 00:21:11,620
While Underwriters Labs make sure things perform safely,

372
00:21:11,720 --> 00:21:14,140
Consumers Union in Yonkers, New York,

373
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:16,840
make sure things perform, period.

374
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:21,340
What we're doing here is the airburst test for condoms.

375
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:23,640
They're like, they go up to about four or five gallons,

376
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:24,740
some of them.

377
00:21:24,840 --> 00:21:25,880
Actually, some of them more.

378
00:21:25,881 --> 00:21:26,780
It's incredible.

379
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:29,280
You would never think that they were that huge.

380
00:21:29,380 --> 00:21:32,380
But I guess it goes to show how strong they need to be in use

381
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:33,280
to be effective.

382
00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:35,820
So I guess it makes sense in some way.

383
00:21:35,920 --> 00:21:37,720
It looks strange, but it makes sense.

384
00:21:37,820 --> 00:21:41,320
The first and foremost of all consumer watchdog groups,

385
00:21:41,420 --> 00:21:45,340
Consumers Union tests an impressive potpourri of products,

386
00:21:45,440 --> 00:21:49,540
everything from running shoes to sports utility vehicles

387
00:21:49,640 --> 00:21:54,740
to fast food to the shirt clips on ballpoint pens.

388
00:21:54,741 --> 00:21:58,880
Unlike Underwriters Labs, which is hired by manufacturers,

389
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:02,220
Consumers Union is completely independent

390
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:05,780
and does its testing for the man on the street.

391
00:22:05,880 --> 00:22:08,320
Products are pitted against advertising claims

392
00:22:08,420 --> 00:22:12,220
and against each other, CU in effect comparison shops

393
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:14,720
for the public, and publishes its results

394
00:22:14,820 --> 00:22:17,500
in Consumer Reports magazine.

395
00:22:17,600 --> 00:22:21,560
Consumers should have the best quality products

396
00:22:21,561 --> 00:22:24,360
for the price they can pay, the fairest price.

397
00:22:24,460 --> 00:22:26,460
Sellers are not interested in consumers

398
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:29,560
having really good information about how products perform

399
00:22:29,660 --> 00:22:31,300
and their true value.

400
00:22:31,400 --> 00:22:33,700
Somebody has to do that for the consumer,

401
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:35,400
and that's what we do.

402
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:38,380
The Consumers Union and Consumers Reports magazine

403
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:41,420
were launched by a group of professors, labor leaders,

404
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:44,620
and journalists in 1936 when the Depression

405
00:22:44,720 --> 00:22:47,460
made it more important than ever for ordinary people

406
00:22:47,560 --> 00:22:49,520
to get their money's worth.

407
00:22:49,521 --> 00:22:52,760
The notion of scientifically testing products against ads

408
00:22:52,860 --> 00:22:55,860
was a new and radical one, and manufacturers

409
00:22:55,960 --> 00:22:57,860
were not at all pleased.

410
00:22:57,960 --> 00:23:01,660
When we first started, the sellers hated us.

411
00:23:01,760 --> 00:23:03,960
They hated us.

412
00:23:04,060 --> 00:23:07,680
We couldn't get our advertisements in the print media.

413
00:23:07,780 --> 00:23:10,980
We were banned from public libraries.

414
00:23:11,080 --> 00:23:14,180
We were telling the truth about products and services.

415
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:16,180
That was unheard of at that time.

416
00:23:16,181 --> 00:23:20,080
And we were basing that not on just one person's opinion,

417
00:23:20,180 --> 00:23:21,680
but on laboratory testing.

418
00:23:21,780 --> 00:23:23,680
We were saying, you know, we bought these products.

419
00:23:23,780 --> 00:23:25,120
We put them in our laboratories.

420
00:23:25,220 --> 00:23:28,420
We subjected them to rigorous scientific testing.

421
00:23:28,520 --> 00:23:30,520
And here are the results.

422
00:23:30,620 --> 00:23:32,520
The sellers didn't like that.

423
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:35,540
With lots of enemies and little funding,

424
00:23:35,640 --> 00:23:39,240
CU's early tests focused on inexpensive products

425
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:41,640
and clever testing schemes.

426
00:23:41,740 --> 00:23:45,680
This 30s test for suntan lotion couldn't have been simpler.

427
00:23:45,681 --> 00:23:47,420
or more effective.

428
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:50,180
Using a UL tester's back as a grid,

429
00:23:50,280 --> 00:23:53,220
16 different creams were tested at a time.

430
00:23:53,320 --> 00:23:56,800
The winners and losers were obvious.

431
00:23:56,900 --> 00:24:00,000
Another classic test was for tissue paper.

432
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:02,200
The sneeze tester is a simple device,

433
00:24:02,300 --> 00:24:06,540
but it takes compressed air, a moist burst of wet,

434
00:24:06,640 --> 00:24:13,400
compressed air, and sneezes itself through a stretched

435
00:24:13,401 --> 00:24:16,700
of tissue, and we would use that as a way of determining

436
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:21,840
the wet strength under pressure of a facial tissue.

437
00:24:21,940 --> 00:24:24,680
By the 1960s, consumers union, which

438
00:24:24,780 --> 00:24:28,380
refused to accept test freebies from manufacturers,

439
00:24:28,480 --> 00:24:32,320
had developed a national network of anonymous shoppers

440
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:36,520
and was testing a much broader range of products.

441
00:24:36,620 --> 00:24:39,660
Some tests were scientifically stringent.

442
00:24:39,661 --> 00:24:43,560
Others, like this one for boys' shoes, were more carefree.

443
00:24:47,160 --> 00:24:49,900
The success of Consumers Reports magazine

444
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,900
puts CU on sound financial footing.

445
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,680
But the organization still devotes a great deal

446
00:24:55,780 --> 00:24:59,380
of its resources to testing simple everyday goods,

447
00:24:59,480 --> 00:25:08,380
like garbage bags or suitcases or spot removers.

448
00:25:08,381 --> 00:25:12,480
The stains that we have here are red wine, chocolate syrup,

449
00:25:12,580 --> 00:25:18,760
mustard, motor oil, lipstick, tea, spaghetti sauce,

450
00:25:18,860 --> 00:25:20,500
and three types of ink.

451
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:22,760
And they're all stains that are considered

452
00:25:22,860 --> 00:25:24,500
very difficult to remove.

453
00:25:24,600 --> 00:25:27,500
In this test, CU put seven different stains

454
00:25:27,600 --> 00:25:30,040
on four different types of fabric,

455
00:25:30,140 --> 00:25:34,080
then tested nine different popular stain removers,

456
00:25:34,081 --> 00:25:38,180
timing them to see if and how quickly they worked.

457
00:25:38,280 --> 00:25:40,420
If the stain is completely removed,

458
00:25:40,520 --> 00:25:43,120
we will score it as very effective.

459
00:25:43,220 --> 00:25:47,320
If there's just a shadow, we'll call it somewhat effective.

460
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:50,660
Anything else is not effective on that stain.

461
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:53,360
Even such a simple test requires

462
00:25:53,460 --> 00:25:56,200
a lot of preparation and follow-up.

463
00:25:56,300 --> 00:26:00,040
The exact same size spot and spot remover measured

464
00:26:00,140 --> 00:26:04,080
to the drop must be put on each piece of cloth.

465
00:26:04,081 --> 00:26:07,020
The painstaking process takes three months

466
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:11,320
and yields 300 pages of data, out of which a statistical model

467
00:26:11,420 --> 00:26:14,720
will be formed and recommendations made.

468
00:26:14,820 --> 00:26:18,020
All that just to decide on a Best Buy.

469
00:26:26,740 --> 00:26:29,800
Fire is one of the deadliest and costliest problems

470
00:26:29,801 --> 00:26:34,540
Americans face, killing 4,300 people a year,

471
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:40,820
injuring 27,000 more, and causing $8.1 billion in damages.

472
00:26:40,920 --> 00:26:45,120
Some of the fires set each year, including car fires,

473
00:26:45,220 --> 00:26:49,920
petroleum fires, and home and apartment fires,

474
00:26:50,020 --> 00:26:53,860
are set by the US government, more precisely

475
00:26:53,960 --> 00:26:57,520
by the National Institute of Standards and Technology,

476
00:26:57,521 --> 00:27:01,520
or NIST, which believes quite literally in fighting fire

477
00:27:01,620 --> 00:27:04,160
with fire.

478
00:27:04,260 --> 00:27:07,860
NIST has been conducting a wide range of real world fire tests

479
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:12,740
since 1901, torching objects, rooms, and entire buildings

480
00:27:12,840 --> 00:27:16,440
to study how materials burn and fire spread.

481
00:27:16,540 --> 00:27:20,080
In this test, conducted for the massive Government Services

482
00:27:20,180 --> 00:27:23,680
Administration, NIST ignited a set of office cubicles

483
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:26,920
to see how a typical GSA environment would burn.

484
00:27:26,921 --> 00:27:30,960
We know how small components burn under very well-controlled

485
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:31,860
conditions.

486
00:27:31,960 --> 00:27:34,720
But what happens when you put all those components together,

487
00:27:34,820 --> 00:27:37,200
the computer, the chair, the carpeting,

488
00:27:37,300 --> 00:27:41,860
the materials in the wall, the paneling systems, the desks,

489
00:27:41,960 --> 00:27:44,700
not to mention all the paper and the plastic and everything

490
00:27:44,800 --> 00:27:48,840
else that users bring in, the notebooks, the floppy disks,

491
00:27:48,940 --> 00:27:49,880
all that kind of thing.

492
00:27:49,980 --> 00:27:52,420
We do not have the engineering methods currently available

493
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:55,620
to us today to be able to predict the rate of that fire

494
00:27:55,720 --> 00:27:56,920
spread.

495
00:27:56,921 --> 00:28:01,020
tests to be able to demonstrate how rapidly that fire develops

496
00:28:01,120 --> 00:28:02,860
and what kind of products come off,

497
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:04,660
what kind of hazardous products come off.

498
00:28:08,860 --> 00:28:11,160
NIST itself goes far beyond its walls

499
00:28:11,260 --> 00:28:15,600
in suburban Washington, DC, to choreograph fires.

500
00:28:15,700 --> 00:28:18,500
This 30 by 30 foot pool of petroleum

501
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:21,020
in Chesapeake Beach, Maryland, was set ablaze

502
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:25,480
to test a new kind of foam on oil spills.

503
00:28:25,481 --> 00:28:28,320
This living room in Fort Indian Gap, Pennsylvania,

504
00:28:28,420 --> 00:28:31,420
was burned to demonstrate how quickly a dry Christmas tree

505
00:28:31,520 --> 00:28:34,720
can flame out of control.

506
00:28:34,820 --> 00:28:37,920
Many of NIST's most dramatic and elaborate fires

507
00:28:38,020 --> 00:28:42,840
are set to study a deadly phenomenon known as flashover.

508
00:28:42,940 --> 00:28:46,240
Flashover is a kind of ignition point when gases

509
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:48,840
and temperatures get so hot that everything

510
00:28:48,940 --> 00:28:52,340
in the immediate environment ignites at the same moment.

511
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:54,640
In some of the videos, you can see flashover

512
00:28:54,641 --> 00:28:57,980
in the case of the living room, where a modest ignition

513
00:28:58,080 --> 00:29:01,940
source, an electric match, ignites a sofa,

514
00:29:02,040 --> 00:29:04,820
and you see the flashover occur as the hot upper gas layer

515
00:29:04,920 --> 00:29:07,260
builds and then ignites, and the flames come out

516
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:08,760
of the doorway of the room.

517
00:29:08,860 --> 00:29:13,060
And within 3 minutes and 10 seconds, 3 minutes, 15 seconds,

518
00:29:13,160 --> 00:29:16,460
we see that room going from a room that you could escape from

519
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:19,160
to be a room where anyone in that room would die.

520
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:24,640
There was a survey taken a number of years ago

521
00:29:24,641 --> 00:29:27,640
And a large number of people felt that after the smoke

522
00:29:27,740 --> 00:29:29,880
detector activated, they would have 10 to 15 minutes

523
00:29:29,980 --> 00:29:31,440
to get out of their house.

524
00:29:31,540 --> 00:29:34,220
And many times, we find that we have a flashover anywhere

525
00:29:34,320 --> 00:29:37,880
from two to four minutes after the smoke detector has

526
00:29:37,980 --> 00:29:40,580
activated with most residential furnishings.

527
00:29:40,680 --> 00:29:43,400
So people really don't have that much time.

528
00:29:43,500 --> 00:29:47,000
NIST isn't the only entity playing with fire.

529
00:29:47,100 --> 00:29:49,340
The Consumer Product Safety Commission

530
00:29:49,440 --> 00:29:53,840
tests everything from cigarettes to children's pajamas

531
00:29:53,841 --> 00:29:59,080
to party toys like Happy String, a highly flammable cake

532
00:29:59,180 --> 00:30:03,480
decoration that was recalled in 1999.

533
00:30:03,580 --> 00:30:05,780
Sandia National Laboratories, which

534
00:30:05,880 --> 00:30:08,460
has helped oversee our nuclear weapons program

535
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:14,060
since the 1940s, was behind this 1978 fire test,

536
00:30:14,160 --> 00:30:17,600
in which nuclear waste containers were heated for 90 minutes

537
00:30:17,700 --> 00:30:23,840
at 1,400 degrees to see if they would melt or leak.

538
00:30:23,841 --> 00:30:27,080
They didn't, but just to make sure they were reliable,

539
00:30:27,180 --> 00:30:33,920
Sandia also bounced them from 30 feet,

540
00:30:34,020 --> 00:30:39,620
smashed them into a 690-ton concrete block at 84 miles an

541
00:30:39,720 --> 00:30:43,960
hour, and rammed them with a train.

542
00:30:44,060 --> 00:30:47,660
Remarkably, the containers withstood this punishment

543
00:30:47,760 --> 00:30:48,600
without a crack.

544
00:30:48,601 --> 00:30:53,600
America, victorious in World War II,

545
00:30:53,700 --> 00:30:59,040
must still fight the greatest civilian hazard of all, fire.

546
00:30:59,140 --> 00:31:01,740
Underwriters Laboratories has been burn testing

547
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:06,220
on a large scale for over 50 years, putting everything

548
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:10,020
from glass partitions to roofing shingles fanned

549
00:31:10,120 --> 00:31:15,620
by a 12-mile-per-hour wind through a literal trial by fire.

550
00:31:15,720 --> 00:31:18,600
Through the years, Underwriters has taken

551
00:31:18,601 --> 00:31:22,100
and pioneered many testing techniques.

552
00:31:22,200 --> 00:31:26,180
In this 1940s test, a sprinkler system is triggered,

553
00:31:26,280 --> 00:31:27,880
then measured on a partition floor

554
00:31:27,980 --> 00:31:32,140
to see exactly how much water it delivers throughout the room.

555
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:36,720
Today, Underwriters runs the world's largest indoor fire

556
00:31:36,820 --> 00:31:40,880
testing facility, a mammoth adjustable furnace the size

557
00:31:40,980 --> 00:31:44,700
of a convention hall that was built in 1996.

558
00:31:44,701 --> 00:31:50,700
It measures 120 feet by 120 feet by 55 feet tall.

559
00:31:50,701 --> 00:31:56,700
It has a large movable ceiling, which measures 100 feet by 100 feet, weighs 200 tons.

560
00:31:56,701 --> 00:31:59,700
It's the world's largest land-based elevator.

561
00:31:59,701 --> 00:32:03,700
But here's where we do the very large warehouse simulations.

562
00:32:03,701 --> 00:32:10,700
If we want to simulate a hyperstore with flammable liquids stocked 30 feet in the air, we can do that.

563
00:32:10,701 --> 00:32:12,700
Whiskey barrels filled with whiskey.

564
00:32:12,701 --> 00:32:15,200
We could pull in a mobile home fully furnished

565
00:32:15,300 --> 00:32:16,600
and test it in here.

566
00:32:16,700 --> 00:32:18,640
The UL fire testing compound

567
00:32:18,740 --> 00:32:23,840
consists of 30 state-of-the-art testing chambers.

568
00:32:23,940 --> 00:32:26,120
In this chamber, a fire has been lit

569
00:32:26,220 --> 00:32:29,020
to test a new foam suppressant.

570
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:31,460
The foam, which is still in the development stage,

571
00:32:31,560 --> 00:32:36,860
must douse the flame in seconds to burn the UL seal.

572
00:32:36,960 --> 00:32:41,220
It fails and has to give way to a stronger extinguisher,

573
00:32:41,221 --> 00:32:43,460
which knocks out the fire with ease.

574
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:45,960
In another chamber, boxes of plastic cups

575
00:32:46,060 --> 00:32:47,460
have been assembled.

576
00:32:47,560 --> 00:32:49,700
And we're going to burn that, just a small array,

577
00:32:49,800 --> 00:32:50,900
as we call it here.

578
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,500
And we're going to see how much fire suppression is needed,

579
00:32:53,600 --> 00:32:56,700
how much water is required to suppress that fire

580
00:32:56,800 --> 00:32:57,600
with that commodity.

581
00:33:02,400 --> 00:33:04,640
And that's just a small scale fire.

582
00:33:04,740 --> 00:33:06,180
Many of the fires that we run here

583
00:33:06,280 --> 00:33:09,940
are 50-fold of what you're seeing in this small scale

584
00:33:09,941 --> 00:33:10,840
example here.

585
00:33:16,240 --> 00:33:19,320
The Department of Defense, not surprisingly,

586
00:33:19,420 --> 00:33:21,760
does an enormous amount of weapons and equipment

587
00:33:21,860 --> 00:33:22,820
testing.

588
00:33:22,920 --> 00:33:26,660
Every piece of artillery, every handgun, every vehicle

589
00:33:26,760 --> 00:33:30,260
has to operate without a hitch in the toughest conditions

590
00:33:30,360 --> 00:33:32,360
that war can provide.

591
00:33:32,460 --> 00:33:36,740
We cannot afford in this business of warfare

592
00:33:36,840 --> 00:33:39,200
to have a battlefield recall.

593
00:33:39,201 --> 00:33:43,840
In those conditions, someone would be injured or die.

594
00:33:43,940 --> 00:33:47,800
So the key to testing is to give that independent look,

595
00:33:47,900 --> 00:33:51,780
that quality look, to see that the equipment will meet

596
00:33:51,880 --> 00:33:54,080
the challenge of the battlefield.

597
00:33:54,180 --> 00:33:57,320
So we have developed many procedures

598
00:33:57,420 --> 00:34:02,460
that duplicate the stress that you would see in a battlefield

599
00:34:02,560 --> 00:34:06,460
without having to actually be in the combat zone.

600
00:34:06,461 --> 00:34:10,000
The Army tests its wares in a half dozen proving grounds

601
00:34:10,100 --> 00:34:11,860
around the country.

602
00:34:11,960 --> 00:34:14,060
The oldest and one of the biggest

603
00:34:14,160 --> 00:34:16,800
is in Aberdeen, Maryland, on former farmland

604
00:34:16,900 --> 00:34:19,140
near the Chesapeake Bay.

605
00:34:19,240 --> 00:34:22,180
Created in 1917 when the Army needed room

606
00:34:22,280 --> 00:34:25,140
to test World War I tanks and planes,

607
00:34:25,240 --> 00:34:29,620
this 76,000-acre spread has felt the impact of tens of thousands

608
00:34:29,720 --> 00:34:34,820
of shells and 24 million pounds of bombs.

609
00:34:34,821 --> 00:34:37,160
During the Second World War, Aberdeen

610
00:34:37,260 --> 00:34:40,460
was the busiest proving ground in the world.

611
00:34:40,560 --> 00:34:43,660
Virtually every weapon of the war was tested here,

612
00:34:43,760 --> 00:34:46,700
including tank flotation devices that were crucial

613
00:34:46,800 --> 00:34:52,340
to the success of D-Day and a 280-millimeter atomic cannon

614
00:34:52,440 --> 00:34:56,640
capable of ground firing an atom bomb.

615
00:34:56,740 --> 00:34:59,240
That was about the biggest thing that we tested here, yes.

616
00:34:59,340 --> 00:35:03,480
And we actually did proof firing on the weapon itself.

617
00:35:03,481 --> 00:35:06,280
We, of course, never did fire an atomic round.

618
00:35:06,380 --> 00:35:10,420
You could fire an inert round without any trouble at all

619
00:35:10,520 --> 00:35:14,260
and stay within the boundaries of our capabilities.

620
00:35:14,360 --> 00:35:17,860
And we did fire a number of rounds.

621
00:35:17,960 --> 00:35:21,100
World War II tanks, jeeps, and other vehicles

622
00:35:21,200 --> 00:35:22,960
were also thoroughly tested.

623
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:37,480
Well, right now we're at the Munson test area,

624
00:35:37,580 --> 00:35:39,680
one of three primary automotive test courses

625
00:35:39,780 --> 00:35:41,980
that we have here at Aberdeen Proving Ground.

626
00:35:42,080 --> 00:35:45,720
And this particular area is about 150 acres,

627
00:35:45,820 --> 00:35:50,700
and there's about 25 discrete obstacles or road courses here

628
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:54,960
to determine the characteristics, physical characteristics,

629
00:35:55,060 --> 00:35:57,800
dimensions, and capability of the vehicles.

630
00:35:57,900 --> 00:36:00,580
We'll be seeing a truck going over what's called

631
00:36:00,581 --> 00:36:08,560
We'll be seeing a truck going over what's called our frame twister course, which is a series of sine waves.

632
00:36:09,240 --> 00:36:17,920
And the purpose of that is to take a look at the structural strength of the frames on vehicles and the welds that hold all the frame rails together.

633
00:36:18,100 --> 00:36:25,420
And also to check out the suspensions and their ability to stay on the ground and keep that vehicle moving along.

634
00:36:25,421 --> 00:36:28,160
The work we do on our washboard courses here,

635
00:36:28,260 --> 00:36:31,560
that includes the 6-inch, enters a 2-inch washboard

636
00:36:31,660 --> 00:36:34,400
and a 3-inch space bump.

637
00:36:34,500 --> 00:36:36,700
They're largely instrumented tests

638
00:36:36,800 --> 00:36:40,940
where we add accelerometers to see the input

639
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:43,540
to the sophisticated equipment on board.

640
00:36:43,640 --> 00:36:47,880
We do want to protect it from severe vibration and shock.

641
00:36:47,980 --> 00:36:51,180
Ever wonder what it's like inside an armored vehicle

642
00:36:51,280 --> 00:36:53,020
during a direct hit?

643
00:36:53,021 --> 00:36:56,520
These filmed lethality and armor penetration tests

644
00:36:56,620 --> 00:37:00,660
recording the impact of a shell on fully instrumented dummies

645
00:37:00,760 --> 00:37:03,360
is probably as close as you want to get.

646
00:37:03,460 --> 00:37:07,700
What our concern is in lethality and armor testing

647
00:37:07,800 --> 00:37:11,040
is the killing power of the threat weapon.

648
00:37:11,140 --> 00:37:13,000
Does it give a crew kill?

649
00:37:13,100 --> 00:37:14,940
Does it give a vehicle kill?

650
00:37:15,040 --> 00:37:17,840
One of the biggest dangers inside a vehicle, if it's

651
00:37:17,940 --> 00:37:20,380
perforated, is the fragments.

652
00:37:20,381 --> 00:37:23,080
So we used mannequins just to catch fragments.

653
00:37:23,180 --> 00:37:25,780
They're fully suited, just as a soldier would be.

654
00:37:25,880 --> 00:37:31,320
Obviously, if you're in the zone of the round as a person,

655
00:37:31,420 --> 00:37:32,620
you're going to die.

656
00:37:32,720 --> 00:37:35,060
But the question is, can the rest of the crew survive

657
00:37:35,160 --> 00:37:36,860
and get that vehicle out of harm's way?

658
00:37:42,200 --> 00:37:45,680
Aberdeen is also the site of a great deal of underwater

659
00:37:45,681 --> 00:37:51,020
munitions testing held in an artificially created super pond.

660
00:37:51,120 --> 00:37:55,020
Interestingly, when the pond was dug out in 1996,

661
00:37:55,120 --> 00:37:59,020
engineers found over 450 live bombs

662
00:37:59,120 --> 00:38:03,000
dropped here during World War II tests.

663
00:38:03,100 --> 00:38:06,400
The Navy uses this site to shock test battleships,

664
00:38:06,500 --> 00:38:10,000
that is, to see how electrical and other sensitive systems

665
00:38:10,100 --> 00:38:13,300
react to a powerful explosion at close quarters.

666
00:38:15,680 --> 00:38:19,580
One of the underwater blasts used

667
00:38:19,680 --> 00:38:23,920
to test this minesweeper registered 3.2 on the Richter

668
00:38:24,020 --> 00:38:24,760
scale.

669
00:38:26,660 --> 00:38:30,000
In July 1997, the Navy used the super pond

670
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:33,260
to put a fascinating theory to the test.

671
00:38:33,360 --> 00:38:37,200
Could a wall of water knock a missile off course?

672
00:38:37,300 --> 00:38:41,900
You use a water curtain to defeat an incoming munition.

673
00:38:41,901 --> 00:38:45,980
We actually had charges in the pond

674
00:38:46,080 --> 00:38:50,080
that would produce a curtain when an incoming missile

675
00:38:50,180 --> 00:38:53,680
was coming in, and the idea was to make that missile either

676
00:38:53,780 --> 00:38:56,420
miss or detonate on the water.

677
00:38:56,520 --> 00:39:00,060
A defensive maneuver that's quite interesting

678
00:39:00,160 --> 00:39:02,720
if it could work for the Navy, because obviously they

679
00:39:02,820 --> 00:39:04,080
have a lot of water.

680
00:39:04,180 --> 00:39:06,560
In fact, the curtain did work,

681
00:39:06,660 --> 00:39:10,600
and the Navy is continuing to explore this innovative means

682
00:39:10,601 --> 00:39:11,840
of missile defense.

683
00:39:15,940 --> 00:39:18,600
All military hardware must be weather tested

684
00:39:18,700 --> 00:39:21,980
to make sure it can function at any time of the year

685
00:39:22,080 --> 00:39:24,180
on any part of the planet.

686
00:39:24,280 --> 00:39:27,020
Aberdeen does a good deal of weather testing,

687
00:39:27,120 --> 00:39:30,080
but the military's number one weather testing facility

688
00:39:30,180 --> 00:39:32,620
and the largest site of its kind in the world

689
00:39:32,720 --> 00:39:36,160
is at Eglin Air Force Base in Florida.

690
00:39:36,260 --> 00:39:39,160
Here in the Florida heat, planes and helicopters

691
00:39:39,161 --> 00:39:42,500
are subjected to all manner of climatic extremes,

692
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:46,460
including sandstorms, dust storms, solar radiation,

693
00:39:46,560 --> 00:39:50,500
wind-blown rain, salt fog, and ice and snow.

694
00:39:50,600 --> 00:39:53,340
The temperature ranges we can reach in our chamber

695
00:39:53,440 --> 00:39:57,640
go as high as 165 degrees Fahrenheit

696
00:39:57,740 --> 00:40:01,580
and as low as minus 65 degrees Fahrenheit.

697
00:40:01,680 --> 00:40:06,780
And the reason that we chose those temperature ranges

698
00:40:06,781 --> 00:40:11,780
is those are the extremes you will find here on the Earth.

699
00:40:12,780 --> 00:40:15,780
Eglin was founded in 1944 in the wake of problems

700
00:40:15,781 --> 00:40:19,780
American pilots were having in the icy war zones of Europe

701
00:40:19,781 --> 00:40:21,780
and the monsoons of the Pacific.

702
00:40:21,781 --> 00:40:24,780
If you needed to move your tanks, your troops and all that,

703
00:40:24,781 --> 00:40:28,780
the best time to do it was during adverse weather conditions

704
00:40:28,781 --> 00:40:31,780
because you didn't have to worry about aircraft coming along

705
00:40:31,781 --> 00:40:34,780
and bombing you because the aircraft couldn't get off the

706
00:40:34,781 --> 00:40:40,940
ground in low temperature, snow, rain, the various adverse weather conditions.

707
00:40:40,941 --> 00:40:47,180
So it was decided that the United States military needed a place to test its weapon systems

708
00:40:47,181 --> 00:40:53,980
in these climatic extremes without having to move this equipment around the world to try and find

709
00:40:53,981 --> 00:40:54,740
the weather.

710
00:40:54,741 --> 00:41:00,060
We could have one localized place where you can recreate all the conditions and test

711
00:41:00,061 --> 00:41:01,600
equipment.

712
00:41:01,700 --> 00:41:05,160
Every form of aircraft, including spacecraft,

713
00:41:05,260 --> 00:41:09,640
like this 1971 space capsule used in the Skylab program,

714
00:41:09,740 --> 00:41:14,300
has been tested here since World War II.

715
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:22,120
The B-2 bomber was put through a fierce ice and snow test,

716
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:27,180
as was the F-117 stealth fighter, which was pummeled with wind

717
00:41:27,181 --> 00:41:30,820
with wind and snow in 1991.

718
00:41:30,920 --> 00:41:34,680
After days of this treatment, a one-foot thick layer

719
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:38,420
of snow had to be heat-misted off the jet.

720
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:42,360
You cannot just go out there with a hammer or a pickaxe

721
00:41:42,460 --> 00:41:44,840
or whatever and start chopping away at the ice.

722
00:41:44,940 --> 00:41:47,660
You would severely damage that aircraft.

723
00:41:47,760 --> 00:41:51,500
So you have to find non-destructive ways of removing

724
00:41:51,600 --> 00:41:54,440
all the ice and do it as quickly as possible

725
00:41:54,441 --> 00:41:58,040
so the crew can climb on board and go do their mission.

726
00:41:58,140 --> 00:42:01,640
Revving up the world's largest planes in a closed chamber

727
00:42:01,740 --> 00:42:03,940
is not without its challenges.

728
00:42:04,040 --> 00:42:07,520
Most importantly, the plane has to be securely anchored

729
00:42:07,620 --> 00:42:08,520
to the ground.

730
00:42:08,620 --> 00:42:11,760
If it's pushing forward with 30,000 pounds of thrust,

731
00:42:11,860 --> 00:42:14,920
and we're only holding back with 29,000 pounds,

732
00:42:15,020 --> 00:42:16,300
it's going to start moving.

733
00:42:16,400 --> 00:42:18,440
And we can't allow that to happen.

734
00:42:18,540 --> 00:42:20,860
We can't allow that to happen.

735
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:23,900
What better justification for the whole world

736
00:42:23,901 --> 00:42:30,900
of weather tests, product tests, and crash tests.

737
00:42:37,440 --> 00:42:44,060
So much ingenuity, destruction, and violence,

738
00:42:44,160 --> 00:42:47,700
all aimed at making sure the amazing variety of goods

739
00:42:47,800 --> 00:42:51,400
and services we use won't let us down.

740
00:42:53,900 --> 00:42:56,640
Whatever new products and technology the new century

741
00:42:56,740 --> 00:42:59,300
brings, one thing is certain.

742
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:02,340
The Big Bang universe of crash testing

743
00:43:02,440 --> 00:43:06,640
will itself continue to explode and expand.

